digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N10" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-conclusion: Multiple tumor subtypes show diverse characteristics, and this pattern is common across lineages, therefore PitNETs exhibit high heterogeneity."];
  "N12" [label="(10,0,0) Induction-case: Functional corticotroph tumors demonstrate typical Cushing symptoms, while silent corticotroph tumors demonstrate more aggressive clinical behavior."];
  "N13" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: If specific examples show clinical diversity within subtypes, then this represents a general pattern of intra-subtype heterogeneity."];
  "N15" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If tumors show high heterogeneity within the same classification, then the current classification system has limitations."];
  "N17" [label="(11,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Complex hormone secretion profiles and highly variable clinical outcomes present requirements and challenges for more precise subtype classification."];
  "N18" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If a classification system has limitations in capturing heterogeneity, then it creates requirements for more precise classification methods."];
  "N19" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-conclusion: Complex hormone secretion profiles and highly variable clinical outcomes present requirements for more precise classification, and if classification systems have limitations then they create requirements for more precise methods, therefore the observed heterogeneity necessitates improved classification approaches."];
  "N20" [label="(12,0,0) Deduction-case: Currently, the pathogenesis of most subtypes remains poorly understood and genomic markers for characterization are still lacking."];
  "N21" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If current molecular markers are insufficient, then alternative molecular features should be explored."];
  "N22" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-conclusion: Currently, genomic markers are lacking, and if current molecular markers are insufficient then alternative molecular features should be explored, therefore new molecular features need to be investigated for PitNET classification."];
  "N23" [label="(13,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Previous studies have predominantly focused on exploring genetic features or overall gene expression, overlooking co-transcriptional regulations."];
  "N24" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If previous approaches have overlooked certain molecular mechanisms, then those mechanisms represent unexplored opportunities for classification."];
  "N26" [label="(14,0,0) Deduction-rule: If alternative splicing generates multiple transcripts and diversifies protein products, then it can contribute to molecular diversity and heterogeneity."];
  "N27" [label="(15,1,2) Deduction-case: Currently, alternative splicing has been extensively studied in various human tumors and is implicated in tumor initiation, progression, and treatment resistance."];
  "N29" [label="(15,2,7) Induction-case: Alternative splicing offers invaluable resources for better understanding of inter-tumor heterogeneity and developing new therapies in various cancers."];
  "N30" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: If AS provides resources for understanding heterogeneity in multiple cancer types, then it represents a generalizable approach for tumor classification."];
  "N32" [label="(16,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: The overall landscape of splicing changes in PitNETs has not been thoroughly investigated so far."];
  "N33" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If a mechanism is validated in other tumors but unexplored in a specific tumor type, then investigating it in that tumor type represents a research gap with high potential."];
  "N34" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-conclusion: The landscape of splicing changes in PitNETs has not been investigated, and if validated mechanisms unexplored in specific tumors represent high-potential research gaps, therefore investigating AS in PitNETs addresses a critical knowledge gap."];
  "N39" [label="(19,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: The analysis more faithfully captures the transcriptomic diversity of PitNETs in contrast to TF expression, thereby better demonstrating inter-tumor heterogeneity."];
  "N4" [label="(4,0,0) Deduction-case: Currently, tumors within the same lineage have identical TF expressions but exhibit variations in cellular functions and tumorigenesis."];
  "N40" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If a molecular feature captures diversity more faithfully than existing markers, then it provides superior discriminatory power for classification."];
  "N41" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-conclusion: AS analysis captures transcriptomic diversity more faithfully than TF expression, and if a feature captures diversity better then it provides superior classification power, therefore AS-based analysis offers superior classification capability compared to TF-based approaches."];
  "N42" [label="(20,0,0) Induction-case: Within the TPIT lineage, a distinct splicing regulatory pattern with significant clinical relevance is identified."];
  "N43" [label="(21,0,0) Induction-case: The findings are robustly validated by an independent cohort of 180 pituitary tumors."];
  "N44" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: If specific findings show clinical relevance and are validated independently, then the approach demonstrates practical utility and reproducibility."];
  "N45" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-conclusion: TPIT lineage shows distinct splicing patterns with clinical relevance that are independently validated, and validated clinically relevant findings demonstrate practical utility, therefore the AS-based approach has demonstrated clinical utility and robustness."];
  "N5" [label="(5,0,0) Induction-case: The PIT1 lineage exhibits excessive hormone secretion with multiple tumor types (somatotroph, lactotroph, thyrotroph)."];
  "N6" [label="(6,0,0) Induction-case: Mixed and plurihormonal PIT1 tumors secrete multiple hormones."];
  "N7" [label="(7,0,0) Induction-case: TPIT lineage contains corticotroph tumors and SF1 lineage contains gonadotroph tumors."];
  "N8" [label="(8,0,0) Induction-case: Null cell tumors lack hormone production or specific TF expression, while plurihormonal tumors express multiple hormones with two or more lineages."];
  "N9" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: If multiple tumor subtypes within and across lineages show diverse characteristics, then there exists high heterogeneity in PitNETs."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The study aims to systematically profile alternative splicing events and RNA-binding-protein regulators across bulk and single-cell transcriptomes from pituitary neuroendocrine tumors to uncover subtype-specific splicing signatures, especially clinically relevant TPIT-lineage patterns, and to test whether AS-based signatures better reflect tumor heterogeneity and improve PitNET classification compared with transcription factor–based schemes.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When existing PitNET classification is challenged by tumor heterogeneity and limited molecular markers, and alternative splicing can be profiled across bulk and single-cell transcriptomes to reveal subtype-specific patterns and RBP regulatory networks with clinically relevant TPIT-lineage findings, the study is motivated to develop and evaluate AS-based PitNET classification."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) When tumors within the same transcription-factor lineage share identical TF expression but vary in cellular functions and tumorigenesis, transcription factor–based classification may have limitations for capturing PitNET heterogeneity."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Functional and silent corticotroph tumors illustrate clinically meaningful intra-subtype heterogeneity within corticotroph tumors, including the TPIT lineage context, rather than proving a general pattern across all PitNET subtypes."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because previous PitNET studies predominantly focused on genetic features or overall gene expression and overlooked co-transcriptional regulations, those co-transcriptional mechanisms represent underexplored opportunities for classification research."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Alternative splicing is a tumor-relevant mechanism that can contribute to molecular diversity and heterogeneity, motivating its examination in studies of tumor heterogeneity."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Evidence that alternative splicing provides resources for understanding inter-tumor heterogeneity in various cancers supports investigating AS as a generalizable classification-oriented approach."];
